---
/**
 * /protocol-stack — Overview of the AI-native protocol stack: MCP, HCP, VCP.
 *
 * Hub archetype — chrome + PageHeader + ProtocolStackDiagram + prose reference.
 * Complements the homepage § 01 stack section with a dedicated reference page.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageContainer from '../components/PageContainer.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import ProtocolStackDiagram from '../components/ProtocolStackDiagram.astro';
import { PROTOCOL_PEERS } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Protocol Stack"
  description="The AI-native protocol stack: MCP (capability), HCP (human context), VCP (methodology). Each layer is necessary; none is sufficient on its own."
  path="/protocol-stack"
  ogImage="og-protocol-stack.jpg"
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <PageContainer wide>
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="Reference · The Stack"
      title="The Protocol Stack"
      lede="Three layers, read as peers: capability, context, methodology. Each is necessary; none is sufficient on its own."
      verified="12 May 2026"
      citeUrl="valuecreationprotocol.com/protocol-stack"
    />

    <div class="mb-16">
      <ProtocolStackDiagram />
    </div>

    <article class="prose mx-auto">
      <h2>MCP — Model Context Protocol</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol</a>
        gives AI systems <strong>capability</strong>. It standardizes how language
        models call tools, read external context, and act on the world through
        servers exposing capability surfaces. MCP made AI systems capable of
        operating against arbitrary software substrates without per-vendor
        integration glue.
      </p>
      <p>
        MCP is at one end of the stack because nothing higher matters if the
        models can't reach into the systems where work happens.
      </p>

      <h2>HCP — Human Context Protocol</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={PROTOCOL_PEERS.hcp.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Human Context Protocol</a>
        gives AI systems <strong>human context</strong>.
        It standardizes how AI systems represent and respect the interests,
        agency, preferences, and lived reality of the humans whose work they
        affect. HCP-Lang (March 2026) is the declarative grammar layer that
        lets humans express context as parseable statements rather than
        prompt-injected hints.
      </p>
      <p>
        Capability without human context produces operations that are
        efficient but inhumane — a defect AI-native systems should not perpetuate.
      </p>

      <h2>VCP — Value Creation Protocol</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/positioning">Value Creation Protocol</a>
        gives organizations <strong>methodology</strong>. It encodes the operating
        discipline that translates AI capability and human context into realized
        value for all stakeholders. VCP is the methodology layer — the part
        that prevents capability and context from being applied to the wrong
        problem, or to the right problem with the wrong frame.
      </p>
      <p>
        Capability and context, without methodology, produce efficient progress
        toward poor outcomes. The protocol exists because foundation models default
        to industrial-age operating defaults — leads, funnels, conversion, extraction —
        that VCP-aligned organizations explicitly reject.
      </p>

      <h2>Why three peers</h2>
      <p>
        Each layer is necessary; none is sufficient on its own.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>MCP only:</strong> AI that can act but doesn't know who it's acting for or why.</li>
        <li><strong>HCP only:</strong> AI that respects humans but can't reach the systems where work happens.</li>
        <li><strong>VCP only:</strong> Methodology with no machine-readable substrate or capability surface.</li>
        <li><strong>MCP + HCP only:</strong> Substrate for AI-native operations, but no operating discipline.</li>
        <li><strong>MCP + VCP only:</strong> Methodology that can execute, but doesn't respect human context.</li>
        <li><strong>HCP + VCP only:</strong> Operating discipline that respects humans but can't act on systems.</li>
        <li><strong>All three:</strong> AI-native operations with capability, context, and discipline.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Independence and complementarity</h2>
      <p>
        The three protocols are independent. VCP does not require any particular
        MCP implementation, nor does it mandate a specific HCP. Each layer is
        free to evolve at its own pace, with its own governance, in its own
        community.
      </p>
      <p>
        They are also complementary. A VCP-aligned system that exposes MCP
        servers can be operated by AI agents that respect HCP declarations.
        The three protocols stack cleanly because they were defined at
        different layers of the same problem.
      </p>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/positioning">VCP Positioning Paper</a> — full positioning document</li>
        <li><a href="/encoding-stack">Encoding Stack</a> — Lexicon, VCP-Lang, Value Graph</li>
        <li><a href={PROTOCOL_PEERS.mcp.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">modelcontextprotocol.io</a> — the canonical MCP home</li>
        <li><a href={PROTOCOL_PEERS.hcp.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">humancontextprotocol.com</a> — the canonical HCP home</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </PageContainer>
</BaseLayout>
